    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d72">

                <pb facs="0168" n="66" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg66"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.20/742: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Atherton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-03-19T19:00:00+00:00">March 19, 1935—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 19—3:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>121.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>I am informed by the Foreign Office that when Hitler saw Ambassador Phipps<anchor
                        n="11" xml:id="d72-f1"/> on Saturday he stated in very general terms that
                    Germany insisted upon equality with France on land, in the air and <pb
                        facs="0169" n="67" xml:id="pg67"/> on the sea but that of England, Germany
                    only sought 33 [<hi rendition="#italic">35</hi>] percent of England’s effective
                    naval strength. Foreign Office understands no reference was made to naval
                    strengths in conversations with French and therefore asks the above be regarded
                    as strictly confidential. Simon<anchor n="12" xml:id="d72-f2"/> will raise this
                    naval question on his Berlin visit and point out the futility of ratios and that
                    international naval discussions should be based on programs. If Simon seems to
                    get anywhere with this line of approach to Hitler he will carry further the idea
                    of naval discussions with Germany in London, presumably to begin shortly after
                        Easter.<anchor n="13" xml:id="d72-f3"/> (See paragraph No. 2 of my 84, March
                    1, 4 p.m.)<anchor n="14" xml:id="d72-f4"/> Craigie<anchor n="15" xml:id="d72-f5"
                    /> understands Tokyo hopes for a naval conference as early as June and
                    Matsudaira had recently asked as to when the Foreign Office proposed opening
                    further discussions. Great Britain pointed out no further steps were possible
                    until the result of Yamamoto’s conversations on his return to Japan were known
                    since the Foreign Office had gone so [<hi rendition="#italic">as</hi>] far as it
                    could without knowing how far Tokyo was prepared to meet British proposals of
                    last autumn.<anchor n="16" xml:id="d72-f6"/>
                </p>
                <p>Foreign Office considers this last German declaration may temporarily stiffen
                    Japanese attitude.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Atherton</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
